00:21:36.119 --> 00:21:43.880
It's not like, now I'm the person I'm always gonna be in the chapter that's always gonna exist in these ingredients of life that will never change.
并不是说，现在我成了我永远会成为的那个人，进入了永远存在的章节，拥有了永远不会改变的生活元素。

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.160
No, life is these chapters.
不，生活就是由这些章节组成的。

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:50.799
And we all, we're sold this fairy tale and it's all gonna work out and we're gonna meet the one.
我们所有人都被灌输了这种童话故事，一切都会顺遂，我们会遇到真爱。

00:21:50.799 --> 00:21:54.759
But what if the one, what if we live past that chapter?
但是，如果真爱……如果我们活过了那一章呢？

00:21:54.759 --> 00:21:56.640
Can there be another one?
还会有另一章吗？

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:58.079
Can there be another whole adventure?
还会有另一段完整的冒险吗？

00:21:58.079 --> 00:22:03.200
Can there be another whole life past what you thought was the solution to all of your problems?
在度过你以为能解决所有问题的那段时期之后，还会有另一段完整的生活吗？

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.660
And you know, we were playing with those kinds of ideas.
你知道，我们当时在尝试这些想法。

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:07.960
It didn't hit everyone.
它并没有触动所有人。

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:13.160
Some people were really angry at that and some people really felt seen by that even at the time.
有些人对此真的很生气，而即使在当时，也有人真的从觉得被它理解。

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:19.839
And now it's interesting to have 12 years of hearing people live life and think about it in their life.
现在很有意思的是，12 年过去了，听到人们经历生活，并在生活中思考它。

00:22:19.839 --> 00:22:28.759
And we're, again, so grateful to hear from you about it, Jessica, like, but it seems like I'd love to hear more about what it's been like in this past year for you too.
杰西卡，我们再次非常感谢你跟我们谈论这些，但是听起来，我也很想多了解一下你过去这一年的生活是什么样的。

00:22:28.759 --> 00:22:32.400
I mean, I have so many questions for you, but what is this past year?
我的意思是，我对你有很多问题，但这一年是怎样的？

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:34.920
Who are you a year ago and who are you now?
一年前的你是谁，现在的你又是谁？

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:37.930
And what is that grief journey been like?
那段悲伤的旅程是怎样的？

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:41.130
Ooh, that's a big question.
哦，这是一个很大的问题。

00:22:43.049 --> 00:22:44.369
Who am I now?
现在的我是谁？

00:22:44.369 --> 00:22:48.369
Well, you know, so I have a son, I have a five-year-old son.
嗯，你知道，我有个儿子，我有一个五岁的儿子。

00:22:48.369 --> 00:22:54.769
So it's transitioning from being a family to being a single parent.
所以这是从一个家庭转变为单亲家庭。

00:22:54.769 --> 00:22:57.130
So that's been a big change.
所以这是一个巨大的变化。

00:22:57.130 --> 00:23:15.049
And just, I think the biggest thing is, and I'm still doing it, but finding who you are, and all of this, when you're with someone for eight years, even though I had a life before I met my husband because I didn't get married till my mid 30s, We didn't really get together until that time.
而且我认为最重要的事情是——我还在经历这个过程——当你和一个人在一起八年时，去寻找你是谁，尽管我在遇到我丈夫之前也有过生活，因为我直到三十岁出头才结婚，我们直到那个时候才真正走到一起。

00:23:18.009 --> 00:23:21.529
You can't really go back to the person you used to be.
你真的无法回到以前的那个自己了。

00:23:21.529 --> 00:23:27.650
You've kind of become this person in your marriage and you become a family and you become this unit.
你在婚姻中不知不觉成了这个人，你们变成了一个家庭，变成了一个整体。

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:31.130
And then it just swiftly changes.
然后它就迅速地改变了。

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:39.609
And if you look at things, I think the hardest thing for me was like going to the doctor's office and changing my status to widow.
如果你看一些事情，我想对我来说最难的是去医生的办公室，把我的状态改成“遗孀”。

00:23:39.609 --> 00:23:48.349
which seems like such an archaic, simple thing, but it was hard for me to see myself that way.
这看起来是一个非常古老、简单的称呼，但我很难以那种方式看待自己。

00:23:48.349 --> 00:23:51.450
It really does sound like the 1800s or something, yes.
听起来确实像是 19 世纪的词或者别的什么，是的。

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:55.690
Yeah, but it's like, I'm not a couple, I'm not a thing.
对，但这就像，我不再是一个伴侣，我不再是一个组合。

00:23:55.690 --> 00:24:06.049
I'm just, that's, that's, you know, right, like that, but even though I don't have to label myself that way, those little things were really hard for me.
我只是，那是，你知道的，对吧，尽管我不必给自己贴上那样的标签，但那些小事对我和来说真的很艰难。

00:24:06.049 --> 00:24:43.859
But if it feels like a transformation, I describe to people, and I'm usually a super positive person, but you get into really dark places when you go through grief, and I haven't really experienced grief in this capacity in my life before, you know, I've lost like, and I've lost a grandparent, but this is very different on your psyche because I think your brain wants the person to be there and you think they're there, but your body and your brain do these crazy things.
但如果它感觉像是一种转变，我向人们描述——我通常是一个超级乐观的人，但当你经历悲伤时，你会陷入非常黑暗的境地，而且我以前在生活中从未以这种程度经历过悲伤，你知道的，我失去过，我失去过祖父母，但这在你的心理上是完全不同的，因为我认为你的大脑希望那个人还在那里，你觉得他们还在，但你的身体和大脑会做出这些疯狂的事情。

00:24:43.859 --> 00:25:00.859
So you make choices and decisions and you're like, I don't know if that was the right decision, but I feel like one of the things I was doing, which is part of my personality, is I just wanted to be social because I felt like connecting with people and being with people is the best thing.
所以你做出了选择和决定，你会想，我不知道这是否是正确的决定，但我觉得我在做的事情之一——这也是我性格的一部分——就是我只想社交，因为我觉得与人联系、和人们在一起是最好的事情。

00:25:00.859 --> 00:25:02.563
a total opposite of that.
完全相反的情况。

00:25:02.563 --> 00:25:06.447
They would like, you know, be alone, not talk to anybody.
他们会，你知道，独处，不跟任何人说话。

00:25:06.447 --> 00:25:13.533
And so one of those things was like this podcast, I'm going to call this podcast and tell them my story.
所以其中一件事就像这个播客，我要给这个播客打电话，告诉他们我的故事。

00:25:13.533 --> 00:25:15.032
I'm going to see this.
我要看看这个。

00:25:15.032 --> 00:25:24.980
And I don't know what propelled me to really be that open, but I just felt like sharing that and I guess with artists too, like made me feel good.
我不知道是什么促使我如此敞开心扉，但我只是觉得分享这些——我想对艺术家来说也是如此——让我感觉很好。

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:27.570
Yeah, so this year's been interesting.
是的，所以这一年很有趣。

00:25:27.569 --> 00:25:31.329
I feel like I'm in a more steady place now, but it changes, you know?
我觉得我现在处于一个更稳定的状态，但它会变，你知道吗？

00:25:32.609 --> 00:25:39.890
It's one of those things where you can look at a tree and like you just start crying, for no reason, because that tree reminds me of that for some reason.
这是那种你会看着一棵树然后就开始哭泣的情况，毫无理由，因为那棵树由于某种原因让我想起了那些事。

00:25:42.289 --> 00:25:44.849
But that's, and I've learned like that's just how it works.
但这就是……我已经了解到这就是它的运作方式。

00:25:44.849 --> 00:25:51.529
And I've probably read so many books on grief and all kinds of podcasts on grief.
我可能读过很多关于悲伤的书，听过各种关于悲伤的播客。

00:25:51.529 --> 00:25:53.049
But it's, yeah.
但确实是这样。

00:25:53.049 --> 00:25:56.140
So it's a journey for sure.
所以这绝对是一段旅程。

00:25:56.140 --> 00:26:17.339
You know, in moments of deep grief in my own life, and I've also talked to other people who've experienced deep loss, that there's a, you know, beneath the shattering and the tragedy and the hurt, like there's something really incredible about weeping at the sight of a tree, right?
你知道，在我自己生命的深切悲伤时刻，我也和经历过深切失落的其他人在聊过，在崩溃、悲剧和痛苦的背后，有某种真正不可思议的东西，比如看到一棵树而痛哭，对吧？

00:26:17.339 --> 00:26:30.000
That makes you feel like you're actually like more alive, like more awake, more alert, more sensitive to how hard it is to be a human being, your empathy expands.
这让你觉得自己实际上更有活力，更清醒，更警觉，对作为一个人类是多么艰难更敏感，你的同理心扩展了。

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.019
I mean, there's, I love Nick Cave, I don't know if you know Nick Cave who's a, yeah, yeah.
我的意思是，有，我喜欢尼克·凯夫（Nick Cave），我不知道你知不知道尼克·凯夫，他是，对，对。

00:26:35.019 --> 00:26:41.420
So he has these Red Hand Files that he writes these weekly emails and he lost two children.
所以他有这些“红手档案”（Red Hand Files），他每周写这些电子邮件，他失去了两个孩子。

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:46.299
And he has a book called Faith Hope and Carnage, which if you haven't read, I recommend it.
他有一本书叫《信仰、希望与残暴》（Faith Hope and Carnage），如果你没读过，我推荐这本书。

00:26:46.299 --> 00:26:49.819
You can listen to it too, it's just conversations with him and this Irish journalist.
你也可以听有声书，就是他和这位爱尔兰记者的对话。

00:26:49.819 --> 00:26:55.380
And a lot of it is about grief and art and creativity, but most of it's about grief.
其中很多内容是关于悲伤、艺术和创造力的，但大部分还是关于悲伤。

00:26:55.380 --> 00:27:07.569
And he said this thing that I think about all the time when his son died, his teenage son, he thought he was entering this incredibly small, tragic circle of grieving people.
他说过一句话，我一直都在想，当他的儿子去世时——他的十几岁的儿子——他以为自己正在进入一个极其微小的、充满悲剧色彩的悲伤人群圈子。

00:27:07.569 --> 00:27:12.289
And then he realized he was entering this enormous community of grieving people.
然后他意识到，自己实际上是进入了一个庞大的悲伤人群社区。

00:27:12.289 --> 00:27:21.970
That there were so many people that had lost people that had hit various bottoms in their lives that were just soaked in grief.
有那么多的人失去了亲人，生活跌入了各种谷底，他们都浸泡在悲伤之中。

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:27.170
And he realized he had actually like entered the human family in a different way.
他意识到自己其实是以一种不同的方式加入了人类大家庭。

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.849
And that it wasn't this isolating event.
这并非是一次孤立的事件。

00:27:29.849 --> 00:27:34.289
It was more of a like joint, it knit him to other people.
它更像是一种连结，将他与其他人缝合在了一起。

00:27:34.289 --> 00:27:36.250
Did you have some of that?
你也有过这种感觉吗？

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:37.369
Yes.
是的。

00:27:37.369 --> 00:27:46.250
I joined a grief group actually, just for young spouses, like people who have had young spouses that died, is probably one of the best things I've ever done.
我实际上加入了一个悲伤互助小组，专门针对年轻的配偶，比如经历过年轻配偶去世的人，这可能是我做过的最好的事情之一。

00:27:47.809 --> 00:28:02.609
I didn't know how it would be, but and there's all people from all different backgrounds, but it feels like you're in this club that you don't really wanna be in, but like you're so grateful that you are in a way.
我以前不知道情况会怎样，但里面有来自各种不同背景的人，感觉就像你加入了一个你其实不想加入的俱乐部，但某种程度上你又无比感激自己加入了。

00:28:02.609 --> 00:28:13.200
And it's a lot like you said, Josh, like it's so, you do see the world in the whole new way and I described it to someone recently as seeing the world in 360.
这很像你说的，乔什，这太……你确实以一种全新的方式看待世界，我最近向一个人描述为以 360 度全景看待世界。

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:17.279
Like I kinda just see everything from all sides now.
我现在基本上是从各个角度看待一切。

00:28:17.279 --> 00:28:31.200
And this is coming from a person that's, I'm incredibly empathetic, I think, and very open to a lot of ideas and people, but I just see pain differently, every day struggles differently.
这是一个富有同理心的人的肺腑之言，我认为，我非常乐于接受许多想法和不同的人，但我看待痛苦的方式不同了，看待日常挣扎的方式也不同了。

00:28:33.119 --> 00:28:41.599
Especially when someone dies that you, and everyone feels this way, I'm sure, about people they love who died, but he was someone who really deserved to live longer.
特别是当一个你爱的人去世时——我相信每个人对他们所爱之人的离去都有这种感觉——但他是一个真正值得活得更久的人。

00:28:41.599 --> 00:28:46.486
like he deserved to live this life on earth and he's not anymore.
就像他应该在这世上活下去一样，但他却不在了。

00:28:46.486 --> 00:28:52.350
And it's not only do I have to reconcile that for myself, but also for my son.
我不仅必须为自己接受这一点，还要为我的儿子接受这一点。

00:28:52.350 --> 00:29:09.416
And so it's, there's those things too, but it's a journey, you know, and I don't know how my son, I think about my son too and how he's gonna evolve over time too without having his father, what that means for me in the future.
所以，也有这些事情，但这是一段旅程，你知道的，我不知道我的儿子……我也会想到我的儿子，以及他在没有父亲的情况下将如何随着时间成长，这对我未来意味着什么。

00:29:09.416 --> 00:29:14.980
And to segue back to the show, I think it's kind of beautiful and amazing.
为了把话题切回节目，我觉得这既美丽又神奇。

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:21.896
I thought to myself, how wonderful would it be that I went back to the first person I loved?
我心里想，如果我能回到我爱的第一个人身边，那该有多棒啊？

00:29:21.896 --> 00:29:27.084
Like, that was a person I ended up with, like, he goes back to Robin.
就像，那是我最后在一起的人，就像，他回到了罗宾身边。

00:29:27.084 --> 00:29:29.264
Like, how, how lucky is that?
这得有多幸运啊？

00:29:29.264 --> 00:29:37.759
Like, I think it's to have that in his life, like, it's to go back to his the first person that really knows him.
就像，我认为在他的生活中拥有这个，回到那个真正了解他的最初的人身边。

00:29:37.759 --> 00:29:41.559
Like that's kind of a beautiful ending actually.
这其实是一个相当美好的结局。

00:29:41.559 --> 00:29:42.559
Yeah.
是的。

00:29:42.559 --> 00:29:44.759
So it does kind of wrap it up kind of neatly.
所以它确实算是一个相当圆满的收尾。

00:29:44.759 --> 00:29:45.759
Yeah.
是的。

00:29:45.759 --> 00:29:52.940
And he nearly and messily at the same time.
既圆满又一团糟，同时兼而有之。

00:29:52.940 --> 00:30:18.440
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:21.680
What we're also talking about, we're talking about lots of different things.
我们也在讨论的，我们在谈论很多不同的事情。

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:28.759
But one of the things that I think you're speaking to is that what Craig was talking about, we change.
但我觉得你正在表达的一点是，正如克雷格（Craig）所说的那样，我们在变。

00:30:28.759 --> 00:30:30.119
There's no static version of us.
我们没有静态的版本。

00:30:30.119 --> 00:30:31.599
There's no frozen version of us.
我们没有凝固的版本。

00:30:31.599 --> 00:30:32.560
always changing.
我们总是在变。

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:41.384
But also, as we change the art we love changes with us, if it's good art, I think there is some art that is just like, that's what it is, you know?
但同时，随着我们的改变，如果我们遇到的是优秀的艺术，我们所热爱的艺术也会随之改变，我想有些艺术就是那种“它就是那样”的东西，你知道吗？

00:30:41.384 --> 00:30:51.829
But I don't know if you've had this experience, I certainly have where a book I loved when I was younger, I read it 20 years later, and it just doesn't resonate the same way.
但我不知道你是否有过这种经历，我肯定有过，我年轻时喜欢的一本书，20 年后再读，它就是无法引起同样的共鸣。

00:30:51.829 --> 00:30:56.451
It just, it hit 17 year old me in a way it's not going to hit 37 year old me.
它触动 17 岁时的我的方式，已经无法再去触动 37 岁时的我了。

00:30:56.451 --> 00:31:02.333
And conversely, art that did not make any sense to me when I was younger or baffled or angered me.
反过来，那些在我年轻时完全无法理解、或者让我困惑和生气的艺术。

00:31:02.333 --> 00:31:10.437
When I'm older, I've had enough experience with grief, loss, failure, setback, struggle that it moves me in a completely different way.
当我年纪大了以后，我对悲伤、失落、失败、挫折和挣扎有了足够的体验，它就会以一种完全不同的方式触动我。

00:31:10.437 --> 00:31:24.964
So I think that it's really, I just love your insight that, you know, you don't sound like one of the storm, the barricades, how I met your mother finale people, like, bitch forks outside of CVS, like you don't sound like one of those people.
所以我觉得，我真的非常喜欢你的洞察，你知道的，你听起来不像那种冲上街头、手举路障、抗议《老爸老妈浪漫史》大结局的人，就像在 CVS 超市外挥舞草叉的人一样，你听起来不像是那种人。

00:31:24.964 --> 00:31:28.385
There were those people that continue to be those people.
确实有那样的人，并且一直在那里。

00:31:28.385 --> 00:31:35.048
But you did say that you were kind of angered and annoyed and confused, would you say, or like, I wasn't angry.
不过你确实说过你当时有点生气、恼火和困惑，你会这么说吗，或者说，我其实没生气。

00:31:35.048 --> 00:31:38.049
I mean, I don't think I could ever get that angry.
我的意思是，我觉得我不可能那么生气。

00:31:38.049 --> 00:31:40.250
But you were kind of confused by it.
但你当时确实对此感到有点困惑。

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:41.655
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对，对。

00:31:41.655 --> 00:31:47.890
Or just like it wasn't the, it didn't land where you maybe wanted it to or hoped it to in that moment.
或者只是觉得它不是那种，它在当时可能没有达到你想要或希望的效果。

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:49.430
Yeah.
对。

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:52.238
And I think disappointed is a good word.
而且我觉得“失望”是一个很好的词。

00:31:52.238 --> 00:31:59.398
But I think that's how, but I realized that I I think that's how you're supposed to feel a little bit.
但我觉得这，但我意识到，我想这正是你当时本该有的一点感受。

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:00.250
Well, sure.
嗯，当然。

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:06.289
I mean, when someone dies that you love unexpectedly, how else would you feel?
我的意思是，当一个你爱的人意外离世，你还能有什么别的感受呢？

00:32:06.289 --> 00:32:08.369
I mean, disappointment is a mild word for it.
我的意思是，失望这个词对它来说都有点轻描淡写了。

00:32:08.369 --> 00:32:09.210
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:16.650
But you know, you feel, I think some of the fans were actually having like a weirdly appropriate response, which is they felt robbed.
但你知道，你会有这种感觉，我觉得有些粉丝其实做出了一种诡异而贴切的反应，那就是他们觉得自己被剥夺了。

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:17.650
Yeah, right.
对，没错。

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:21.049
They felt robbed of their happy ending, but you know who else did was Ted.
他们觉得自己被剥夺了本该属于他们的圆满结局，但你知道还有谁也是这样吗？是泰德。

00:32:21.049 --> 00:32:21.601
Yes.
是的。

00:32:21.601 --> 00:32:22.289
Yeah.
对。

00:32:22.289 --> 00:32:23.130
Yes.
是的。

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:23.970
You know.
你知道的。

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:25.690
And it took him a long time.
而且他花了很长时间。

00:32:25.690 --> 00:32:28.250
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对，对，对。

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:38.710
And the whole show when you look at it through that lens is a father preparing to ask his children this incredibly vulnerable thing.
当你从这个角度来看整部剧时，它其实就是一个父亲在做准备，准备向他的孩子们问出一个极其脆弱的问题。

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:49.630
Like, are you okay if I give it another shot with this woman that obviously you know I have always loved and is in our lives.
比如，如果我和这个你们显然知道我一直爱着、并且也在我们生活中的女人再试一次，你们会介意吗？

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:51.710
And, you know, he's really prepared.
而且，你知道，他真的做好了心理准备。

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:56.650
The whole show looked at through that lens is like this achingly beautiful thing, Craig.
克雷格，如果从这个角度来看整部剧，它就像是一件让人心碎的美丽事物。

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:57.750
I just, I don't, I mean.
我只是，我不知道，我的意思是。

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:08.190
Well, he's asking, the whole story is to, is leading to a question with, to his kids, which is, is this okay with you guys?
嗯，他问的是，整个故事都在导向一个向他的孩子们提出的问题，那就是：你们觉得这样可以吗？

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:09.670
Is it okay for me to love again?
我还可以重新去爱吗？

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:11.390
Is it okay for me just to try again?
我还可以再尝试一次吗？

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:19.029
Is it okay to see if that's there for me and this way with this person who you know and you love and is family to us in a way?
我可以看看自己是否还能以这种方式，和这个你们认识、爱戴并且对我们来说就像家人一样的人在一起吗？

00:33:19.029 --> 00:33:22.509
And has been, and is it okay?
一直以来都是如此，这样可以吗？

00:33:22.509 --> 00:33:27.869
is it okay to do that and his kids want him to be happy so they say yes.
这样做可以吗？而他的孩子们希望他快乐，所以他们说可以。

00:33:27.869 --> 00:33:36.829
And in that sense, that's what the story was leading up to for that, you know, that was the way we were approaching the whole series in a way.
从某种意义上说，这就是故事一直在为之铺垫的方向，你知道的，这就是我们处理整部剧的方式。

00:33:36.829 --> 00:33:41.670
And some, yeah, some folks really got that and some folks really didn't.
而且有些，是的，有些人真的理解了，但有些人确实没有理解。

00:33:42.910 --> 00:33:43.829
It's really interesting.
这真的很有趣。

00:33:43.829 --> 00:33:47.750
I mean, Josh and I went through so much, so much in the nine years we made that show.
我是说，乔希和我制作那部剧的九年里，经历了太多太多。

00:33:47.750 --> 00:34:04.630
I wanted to share this with you, Jessica, that my son was born between year two and three, and he was born with life-threatening congenital heart disease, and he had four different heart problems that required open heart surgery when he was two weeks old, and there was about 50, 50 chance of survival.
我想跟你分享这个，杰西卡，我的儿子是在第二年到第三年之间出生的，他出生时患有危及生命的先天性心脏病，他有四种不同的心脏问题，在两周大时就接受了开心手术，当时有大约百分之五十的生存几率。

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:07.549
And he's 18 now, and he's doing great.
他现在18岁了，而且过得很好。

00:34:07.549 --> 00:34:16.230
But in between year and two and three of the show, I thought it was our first born child, and we thought we might lose him.
但在制作该剧的第二年和第三年之间，我们以为他是我们的第一个孩子，我们当时以为可能会失去他。

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:26.110
And just the way that kind of for me at least colored, the way I viewed the show and approached the show and Carter too, he was so in that with me, Josh was so in that with me.
那种经历——至少对我和卡特来说——为我如何看待这部剧、如何对待这部剧涂上了某种色彩，他当时也深深地和我经历着这一切，乔希也深深地和我经历着这一切。

00:34:26.110 --> 00:34:31.750
It was just like this really big life thing was happening alongside our sitcom.
就好像在我们的情景喜剧之外，正在发生一件非常重大的生活事件。

00:34:31.750 --> 00:34:41.510
And these collaborators and dear friends like Josh and like Carter and Pam Freeman and all the whole and everybody like they were there with me on that journey.
这些合作者和亲爱的朋友，像乔希、卡特、帕姆·弗里曼以及所有人，他们都在那段旅程中陪伴着我和我。

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:48.510
They saw me and my wife and Elliot comes through this really, really life for death and then life for death.
他们看着我和我妻子以及埃利奥特经历了一场生死关头，然后又是生死关头。

00:34:48.510 --> 00:34:50.789
And then, well, he lived, now what?
然后，嗯，他活下来了，接下来呢？

00:34:50.789 --> 00:34:52.150
Now what's life?
接下来的人生是什么样的？

00:34:52.150 --> 00:35:04.949
And it just, I don't know, it put us on a different trajectory and obviously the fact, when I heard in your message that your husband had this lifelong congenital heart problem that eventually took him, right?
而且这，我不知道，它把我们带上了一条不同的轨迹，显然，当我听到你在留言中提到你的丈夫有这种终身的先天性心脏病，并最终夺去了他的生命时，对吧？

00:35:04.949 --> 00:35:11.389
And I felt that so much too because that's my son too and my son has lifelong learning disabilities.
我对此也深有感触，因为那也是我的儿子，我的儿子有终身的学习障碍。

00:35:11.389 --> 00:35:12.469
He was a rare genetic syndrome.
他患有一种罕见的遗传综合征。

00:35:12.469 --> 00:35:14.309
The heart was just part of it.
心脏问题只是其中的一部分。

00:35:14.309 --> 00:35:21.070
And it's just, for me, that was the moment my life diverged into a different story than the one I thought, right?
对我来说，那一刻就是我的人生分叉到一个与我原本想象的完全不同的故事中的时刻，对吧？

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:23.429
Which I know is how it feels to lose somebody, right?
我深知失去一个人是什么样的感觉，对吧？

00:35:23.429 --> 00:35:27.070
There was, in a way, a loss of the version of parenthood.
从某种意义上说，我失去了一种为人父母的愿景。

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:28.789
I thought we were gonna have, right?
我原本以为我们会拥有的那种愿景，对吧？

00:35:28.789 --> 00:35:34.949
I was, there was a loss of a plan, a planned life, a planned life I was supposed to have that I ordered up out of the catalog.
我失去了原本的计划，一种我从目录里精心挑选好的人生规划、我本该拥有的人生。

00:35:34.949 --> 00:35:36.150
I wasn't gonna have that life.
我将不再拥有那种生活。

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:39.829
He, my son wasn't gonna have that life and my wife wasn't gonna have that life.
他、我的儿子将不会拥有那种生活，我的妻子也将不会拥有那种生活。

00:35:39.829 --> 00:35:44.269
And then the beauty is, okay, what do we do now?
而美好之处在于，好吧，我们现在该怎么做？

00:35:44.309 --> 00:35:47.469
And answering that day by day, that's the beauty.
日复一日地去回答这个问题，这就是美好之处。

00:35:47.469 --> 00:35:48.949
That's the beauty of moving on.
这就是继续前行的美好之处。

00:35:48.949 --> 00:35:58.949
And if you can survive that and live in the discomfort of that, you can discover gifts along the way, even as you feel the loss of what you thought it was all going to be.
如果你能熬过这一切，并习惯于那种不适感，你就会在沿途发现生命的馈赠，尽管你依然能感受到失去你原本以为的一切的痛苦。

00:35:58.949 --> 00:36:06.429
And I don't know, I just wanted to share that, that there's a heart issue in the heart of my family too.
我不知道，我只是想分享这一点，我的家庭核心也有一个心脏问题。

00:36:06.429 --> 00:36:10.230
And I don't know, I just feel connected to your story in that way.
而且我不知道，我觉得用这种方式和你的故事紧紧相连。

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:11.429
Thank you.
谢谢你。

00:36:11.429 --> 00:36:11.929
Yeah.
对。

00:36:11.929 --> 00:36:20.342
And I, I knew that a little bit, but I, so it's funny when I started listening to the podcast, again, I had forgotten that story about your son.
其实我之前略有耳闻，但很有趣的是，当我开始听这个播客时，我又把关于你儿子的那个故事给忘了。

00:36:20.342 --> 00:36:27.002
And I kind of remembered it going on, let the show and that the you guys did the fundraisers and things like that.
我隐约记起当年节目进行时发生过这件事，你们还搞了筹款活动之类的事情。

00:36:27.002 --> 00:36:31.851
And then I was like, Oh, yeah, I was like, Oh, wow, yeah, he had similar situation.
